{"qid":"Q853477","names":["Lan Xang"],"title":"Lan Xang","endonym":{"native":"ລ້ານຊ້າງຮົ່ມຂາວ","lang":"lo"},"capsule":"Lan Xang ('Million Elephants and the White Parasol') was a Lao Buddhist kingdom founded by Fa Ngum in 1353, who unified the middle Mekong valley with Khmer military support and established Theravada Buddhism as the state religion. The kingdom reached its greatest extent and cultural florescence under Sourigna Vongsa (r. 1638–95), whose long peaceful reign attracted Dutch trader Gerrit van Wuysthoff and left detailed European accounts. After Sourigna Vongsa's death, succession conflicts split the realm by 1707 into three successor kingdoms — Vientiane, Luang Prabang, and Champasak — effectively ending Lan Xang as a unified polity.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Lan Xang's territorial extent is reconstructed from Lao chronicle traditions, Siamese and Vietnamese diplomatic records, and the Dutch account of van Wuysthoff; core territories along the Mekong are reasonably established, but upland and frontier zones are approximate, as the kingdom exercised tributary rather than administrative control over peripheral areas.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Lan Xang","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Lan-Xang"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Lan Xang","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lan_Xang"}],"britannica":"place/Lan-Xang","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Lan Xang","rulers":[{"qid":"Q532364","name":"Fa Ngum","nativeName":"ຝ້າງູ່ນ","roman":"Fa Ngum","start":1353,"end":1372,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Samsenethai","nativeName":"ສາມແສນໄທ","rom